# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", "Aliasing" and "Back to the counterexample".
id: naive-write-rule
title: Why the naive array rule is wrong
topic: Arrays
level: 3
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Aliasing" and "Back to the counterexample"
tags: arrays, aliasing, soundness, counterexample

task:
  The naive rule treats the cell $a[I]$ as a variable and gives
  $\{Q[a[I] <- E] && def(a[I])\}$ `a[I] := E` $\{Q\}$. Find values that
  break the triple that this rule gives for `a[i] := 1` with the
  postcondition $a[j] = 0$. Then compute the correct precondition with
  ARRAY-ASSIGN.

pre: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[j] = 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: What the naive rule gives
rule: LOGIC
text:
  The naive rule replaces the text $a[i]$ in the postcondition. The
  postcondition is written with $a[j]$, so there is nothing to replace:
vc: {a[j] = 0 && def(a[i])} a[i] := 1 {a[j] = 0}
status: fails
counterexample: a = [4, 0, 9], i = 1, j = 1
why:
  Take the array $4, 0, 9$ and $i = j = 1$. The precondition holds:
  $a[1] = 0$ and position $1$ exists. The write sets $a[1]$ to $1$, so
  $a[j] = 0$ is false afterwards. When $i = j$, $a[i]$ and $a[j]$ name the
  same cell: they are aliases, and substitution on the text of the
  formula cannot see it.

step: Substitution with ARRAY-ASSIGN
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: a[i] :=
calc:
  (a[j] = 0)[a <- a[i |-> 1]]
  = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0

step: Select-store
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, LOGIC
calc:
  a[i |-> 1][j] = 0
  = (j = i && 1 = 0) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)
  = (j = i && false) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)
  = j != i && a[j] = 0
text:
  In the case $j = i$, the read gives the written value $1$, and $1 = 0$
  is false. In the case $j != i$, the read gives the old value $a[j]$.

step: Definedness
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
calc:
  wp(a[i] := 1, Q)
  = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0 && def(a[i])
  = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
vc: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> R1
result: The correct precondition contains $j != i$, which is exactly the condition that the naive rule misses.

summary:
  The two rules differ only by the condition $j != i$. In the
  counterexample $i = j = 1$ this condition is false, so the correct rule
  makes no promise about that execution, which is right, since the
  postcondition fails there.
